\subsection{Human Data Validation}
\label{sec:human_data}

A key concern is whether the evidence hierarchy reflects genuine discourse-level generalization or merely within-LLM stylistic transfer, since both training and OOD evaluation data are generated by Qwen3-8B. We test this by evaluating on ToxicChat~\citep{lin2023toxicchat}, a dataset of 204 human-written jailbreak attempts and 204 benign conversations collected from real LMSYS user interactions, entirely independent of any LLM generation pipeline.

All domain-adapted variants substantially outperform vanilla on human data (F1): JB-MLM 0.842$\pm$0.009, TF-IDF 0.710, scrambled 0.667$\pm$0.030, 9-class 0.654$\pm$0.087, vs.\ vanilla 0.221$\pm$0.055. Every domain-adapted variant exceeds vanilla by at least 3$\times$, refuting the hypothesis that the evidence hierarchy is a Qwen3-8B generation artifact: DAPT on synthetic jailbreak text transfers to human-written attacks.

The relative ordering among domain-adapted variants differs from multi-turn evaluations: JB-MLM leads while classification-supervised variants (9-class, scrambled) are comparable. This reflects the modality mismatch between training and evaluation. ToxicChat contains single-turn direct attacks with no multi-turn escalation structure, so the classification head's regularization of turn-sequence representations provides no advantage. MLM captures token-level distributional properties that transfer to single-turn detection without relying on sequential patterns. TF-IDF's competitive performance (0.710) is consistent with the difficulty-threshold pattern from Section~\ref{sec:cross_attack}: lexical features suffice for structurally simple attacks but collapse on complex ones (ActorAttack F1 = 0.261).

