\paragraph{Variance reversal on human data.}
On synthetic OOD evaluations, classification supervision reduces cross-seed variance 4--20$\times$ relative to MLM. On human data (Table~\ref{tab:human_data}), this pattern reverses: 9-class exhibits the highest variance (std\,=\,0.087--0.135) while JB-MLM exhibits the lowest (std\,=\,0.009--0.010). Classification supervision optimizes representations for the training distribution's structure, stabilizing predictions within the synthetic data manifold; when the test distribution shifts to human-written text, these distribution-specific representations become a source of instability. MLM's unsupervised objective imposes no distribution-specific classification boundaries, producing representations that generalize more uniformly across the style gap. Classification supervision and MLM thus serve complementary roles: the former as a within-distribution regularizer, the latter as a cross-distribution generalizer.

\paragraph{Encoder capacity confound.}
The cross-encoder comparison uses DeBERTa-v3-base (184M parameters) and RoBERTa-base (125M parameters), which differ in both pretraining objective and model capacity.

